# Line-ending policy for a cross-platform team (Windows, macOS, Linux). Without this file, each contributor's local core.autocrlf setting decides how line endings are stored, which produces phantom whole-file diffs and the "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warnings. With it, the repository is the single source of truth and every clone behaves the same regardless of local config. If a file ever does look fully rewritten again, run `git add --renormalize .` once.

# Default: git decides what is text, stores it as LF in the repository, and checks it out with the platform's native ending.
* text=auto

# Spelled out so nothing depends on git guessing.
*.html         text
*.css          text
*.js           text
*.mjs          text
*.json         text
*.md           text
*.svg          text
*.txt          text
*.xml          text
*.webmanifest  text
CNAME          text
LICENSE        text
.nojekyll      text

# Must stay LF everywhere, because a Linux runtime executes them. A CRLF here fails with: bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M
*.sh           text eol=lf
*.bash         text eol=lf
Dockerfile     text eol=lf
.dockerignore  text eol=lf
*.yml          text eol=lf
*.yaml         text eol=lf

# Must stay CRLF everywhere, because Windows executes them.
*.bat          text eol=crlf
*.cmd          text eol=crlf
*.ps1          text eol=crlf

# Binary. Git must never touch the bytes of these, nor try to diff them.
*.png          binary
*.jpg          binary
*.jpeg         binary
*.gif          binary
*.ico          binary
*.webp         binary
*.avif         binary
*.svgz         binary
*.pdf          binary
*.zip          binary
*.gz           binary
*.7z           binary
*.woff         binary
*.woff2        binary
*.ttf          binary
*.otf          binary
*.eot          binary
*.mp3          binary
*.mp4          binary
*.webm         binary
*.mov          binary
*.apk          binary
*.aab          binary
*.jar          binary
*.so           binary
*.dll          binary
*.exe          binary
*.keystore     binary
*.jks          binary
